  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 345 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe axis of the primary upper level trough will pass through the \narea Sunday morning, bringing an end to the unsettled weather \npattern. Lingering snow showers should depart the area before dawn \nSunday morning, though the area will remain colder than seasonal \naverages as the chilly surface high lingers over the area. Very weak \ntransient ridging will try to build in Monday, so we should warm up \na few degrees over Sunday's highs as the surface stationary front \nretreats slightly to the northeast away from the terrain. The next \nPacific trough will approach from the west, and chances for \nsnow showers return to the mountains during the daytime hours \nMonday. This system has once again trended towards a \ndisorganized sprawling system with messy forcing for lift. The \nmountains may manage to pick up another round of light snow but \neven the orographic lift looks less impressive with this system.\nOutside of the mountains, about 50% of ensembles show a dusting\nto an inch of snow falling, while the others remain completely \ndry. Low end PoPs continue Monday night, but the odds of notable\nsnowfall are quite low. \n\nTuesday will remain chilly behind the departing storm system, but we \nshould see more sunshine. More organized ridging is expected to \nstart to build in on Wednesday, pushing temperatures back to \nnear or slightly above seasonal averages for the latter part of \nthe work week. Another system may traverse across the region \nWednesday into Thursday (Thanksgiving), but this again looks \nweak, disorganized, and moisture-starved. For now, low end PoPs \nare present in the mountains but the probability is too low to \ninclude a mention of snow for the plains and valleys.",
